findings.recommendation:
Remove all log statements containing password from production builds. Use BuildConfig.DEBUG to gate Log.* calls so they only execute in development. Better still, use ProGuard/R8 rules to strip Log.d, Log.v, and Log.i calls from release builds entirely. Example proguard-rules.pro entry:
-assumenosideeffects class android.util.Log { public static *** d(...); public static *** v(...); public static *** i(...); }
For data that must be logged (debugging, crash reports), redact sensitive fields before logging — for example, log only the last 4 digits of a credit card number, never the full PAN.

Replace java.util.Random and Math.random() with java.security.SecureRandom for any cryptographic or security purpose (tokens, salts, IVs, nonces, session IDs, password reset codes, OTPs, CSRF tokens). SecureRandom is seeded from the operating system's entropy source and is cryptographically unpredictable. Example:
  SecureRandom rng = new SecureRandom();
  byte[] token = new byte[32];
  rng.nextBytes(token);
